nary director that he  can get one it didn't work so they seem  to be trying to recapture that approach  that serve them very well in the past  now when they got Zack Snyder and they  had you know Man of Steel and there's  some debate now it seems like how much a  b vs the theatrical release at any rate  was what snyder actually wanted to  release two films but i think even if  you want to argue that it was cut down  so that it was less coherent which i  don't question it seemed very much to be  his vision of the movie even if it was a  truncated version so it still felt very  much of his now David Ayer on Suicide  Squad we know massively messed with  across the board on that patty Jenkins  they left her alone to do Wonder Woman  so I think Warner Brothers is starting  to realize that whatever they've meddled  whatever the studio has gotten more  involved with more mandates and more  meddling that's when we've gotten stuff  like Batman or Robin Green Lantern  Suicide Squad that approach is not  working they need to back off they need  to stop imposing these kinds of rigid  gates and expectations if they're going  to continue to try and bring in  directors who want to bring their own  flavor are going to want to do their own  things like Adam McKay clearly wants to  do or like hopefully Matt Reeves will do  with the Batman now to contrast that  with Marvel Marvel brought in I'm not a  visionary might be pushing a little bit  Marvel brought in their people with  stronger specific artistic voices  earlier on the process you got people  like Kenneth Branagh on Thor and as  they've sort of gotten momentum and  moved along they've been turning much  more towards television people that's  where the Russo brothers came out of and  I think that's because Marvel has a very  clear vision and they know what they  want and they just want someone to come  in and give you you know give their  little touches but ultimately deliver  what they've already said they wanted to  be that'